\f0\b\fs48 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cf2 \cb3 CS314: Principles of Programming Languages\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs40 \AppleTypeServices \cf2 Professor: Zheng Zhang\
Ranking: 3.7 (Course Content = 2.5, Professor\'92s style of teaching = 1.2)\
This course explores programming language theory \'97 to critically compare and choose the most appropriate model to solve a particular problem; learning of new languages \'97 languages are constantly changing to keep up with the advances in modeling and expressing computation; and better design of domain-specific languages (DSL) \'97 focus on designing domain-specific API and programming language rather than a general-purpose programming language. Course content covers the basic structure of programming languages, computing models, and language paradigms.\
By the end of the course, you should easily be able to learn new programming languages, how to enable full use of programming languages, understand the implementation of different programming languages, and be able to make thoughtful decisions about the programming language suited to each problem.\
My favorite parts of the course were the programming assignments and the fact that the lowest score out of the midterm or the final would be dropped.\
My least favorite part was that the lectures overlapped a lot. The professor would spend half the lecture reviewing past week\'92s lectures rather than teaching new content every week.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cf2 CS323: Numerical Analysis\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs40 \AppleTypeServices \cf2 Instructor: Eliane Zerbetto-Traldi\
Ranking: 4.5 (Course content = 2.0, Professor\'92s style of teaching = 2.5)\
This course explores the challenges faced with the performance of mathematical problems that are implemented on computers, and how these challenges can be overcome by developing sequences of calculations to a numerical solution which will simultaneously consider the errors. Course content covers traits and trade-offs of direct and iterative methods, error-analysis and computer arithmetic, solutions to linear and non-linear systems of equations, approximation and interpolation, and computational solutions to differentiation and integration problems.\
By the end of this course, you should have learned techniques to develop numerical algorithms to approximate solutions to computational problems, and effectively apply direct and iterative methods to solve problems within the domain of numerical analysis and scientific computing.\
My favorite part of the course was the content. In particular, I found it useful to learn about error-analysis when solving problems numerically and always consider using techniques from this course when working with numerical data.\
My least favorite part of the course was the projects. The projects were too easy and didn\'92t challenge my skill level.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cf2 CS336: Principles of Information and Database Management\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs40 \AppleTypeServices \cf2 Professor: Antonio Miranda\
Ranking: 4.2 (Course Content = 2.5, Professor\'92s style of teaching = 1.7)\
This course explores various languages for representing and accessing the different kinds of information, methodologies for using them, theoretical principles of information and databases, some fundamental algorithms, and working with Database Management Systems (RDBMS). Course content covers information system design \'97 modeling the data; data storage; database models \'97 hierarchical, network, relational, object-oriented; DBMS \'97 functional and relational model.\
By the end of this course, you should be able to design information systems that mimic real-life operations, make quick decisions about the database model that would best fit a problem, and utilize DBMS technologies to effectively store and model the data.\
My favorite part of the course was the semester-long team website development project. This project taught me a lot of useful skills and putting it on my resume helped me to land a great job. Also, the exams were multiple choice, which was nice.\
My least favorite part of the course was that I was stuck with bad team members when the website project demanded effort and great teamwork.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cf2 CS352: Internet Technology\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs40 \AppleTypeServices \cf2 Professor: Richard Martin\
Ranking: 2.5 (Course content = 2.0, Professor\'92s style of teaching = 0.5)\
This course explores the internet. Specifically, what makes the internet work, how it works, and why it works. Course content covers the internet\'92s history, it\'92s layers, the network core and edge, internet protocols, performance, and security.\
By the end of this course, you should be able to understand the lifecycle of sending and receiving information through the internet, how the internet stays active and running, and security principles and applications that make the internet safe for your computer.\
My favorite part of the course was the Wireshark lab and the message transfer assignment. The Wireshark lab was great for learning how information (in packets) moves along the internet. The message transfer assignment was an applied version where you create your own packets to send/receive, and also involved encryption and decryption.\
My least favorite part of the course was the professor\'92s style of teaching. The professor simply dictated the slides and wasn\'92t very engaging, so it became more of a self-taught course.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cf2 CS440: Artificial Intelligence (AI)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs40 \AppleTypeServices \cf2 Professor: Jingjin Yu\
Ranking: 4.5 (Course content = 2.5, Professor\'92s style of teaching = 2.0)\
This course introduces the concept of AI in depth, and how the study of AI enables us to design computational methods that allow cyber and/or physical systems to act autonomously. Course content covers problem-solving by search, game theory, logic and inference, probabilistic reasoning, and machine learning.\
By the end of the course, you should have learned the techniques of modern AI as well as their applications and identify appropriate models and methods for attacking a broad array of complex decision-making problems.\
My favorite parts of the course were probabilistic reasoning and machine learning applications. In particular, I really enjoyed the machine learning assignment. I put it on my resume because it was an awesome project that taught me valuable skills, and it certainly opened doors for me in my career search.\
My least favorite part of the course was that projects were tailored to be completed in pairs, and they were tough individually. I generally like working in a team, but my partner didn\'92t pull the fair share of the weight, so it affected my performance in the projects (My number one rule for teamwork: to find hardworking team members).\
}